The openWorkspace service calls getRecruiterCompany(userId) before inserting, to detect a pre-existing company. Is this check race-condition safe? Describe a scenario where two requests could slip through the check simultaneously and produce duplicate rows. How would you make the guard watertight at the database level?

Consider a scenario where two users are trying to create a company with name x. Now both users when make a request the first part of the operation is to check if a x named company exists or not. Both if simultaneously go through this check it will return false. So both of these would then produced to create a company with name x resulting in a conflict. We can enforce unique company names or atleast slugs to prevent duplication at the data base level. Another option is to reserve the specific name on the first check like lock it so that the other one gets true for the isExisting check.

The company starts with status='pending' and the recruiter can already use all recruiter-facing routes. Compare this to an alternative design where status='pending' blocks job posting. What are the trade-offs for the recruiter's experience and for admin workload?

The first design to allow recruiters to post jobs before verification saves them time for the duration it takes an admin to approve or verify the company. With this the jobs would be there right after verification. But this impacts admin as he now needs to verify the jobs too this can make it easy for fradulent companies to get away without check. On the contrary the recruiters would be bounded by admins approval and then after approval job creation would take time too wasting a lot of time.